    "title": "3 men to stand trial for Easter Sunday Jackson homicide - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Shajaka Shelton ,\n6 hours ago\nLANSING, Mich. (WLNS) — A Jackson County judge ruled three men will stand trial on charges stemming from an Easter Sunday incident in the city of Jackson that resulted in the death of John Lusch, 45, of Marshall.\nBryan Johnson, 21; Jemel Milton, 36; and Phillip Gauze, 40 are all being held without bail at the Jackson County Jail, accused of committing a homicide that happened at home in the 900 block of Orchard Place on April 20, 2025.\nThe Jackson Police Department says officers responded to the home at around 11:30 p.m. for a welfare check. While searching the home, officers found Lusch dead on the scene.\nInvestigators say Lusch went to the home at some point Sunday morning, where he was “confronted” by several individuals in the home and shot multiple times.\nNow, the three men will stand trial on charges of first-degree felony murder, unarmed robbery, and conspiracy to commit unarmed robbery. Johnson and Gauze were bound over on additional charges of tampering with evidence, and Johnson is facing an additional charge of unlawful imprisonment.\nAll three\nAccording to a release from the Jackson County Prosecutor’s Office, at the end of the second day of the preliminary examination, District Court Judge Allison L. Bates found probable cause and bound all three men over to Circuit Court, where they were assigned to Judge John G. McBain.\nCopyright 2025 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to WLNS 6 News.",
